nt users  let's see what happens  uh-huh  looks like a decent amount of operations  per second here  yes  now let's look at the cpu to see how  it's holding up  uh-huh it looks like the load is  crossing the recommended cpu threshold  here uh what do we do  right if it goes much beyond that  threshold you would see request  latencies increase we don't want that  we can just increase the processing  units let's do that now  all right  looks like the cpu is having an easier  time now  and in locust we're able to handle many  more requests per second than before  those extra units really helped pretty  impressive for a globally consistent  relational database with acid  transactions  and nice that you can tweak capacity  while the database is under load  yeah and there are no changes to the  application needed  great the customer will get acid  transactions in a managed database with  spanner they can start small and scale  up without users being impacted thanks  for showing me how to do this derek  you're welcome martin i think spanner  would work well for the next version of  the customer system  yeah i think so thank you everyone for  watching if you have questions for me or  derrick about spanner please enter them  in the comments below also let us know  in the comments if you have suggestions  for other serverless topics we should  bring up on the show  bye for now  [Music]
 

